From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winewayland: Detect HDR independently of Windows image descriptions.

Dragon's Dogma 2 no longer exposes HDR after 58448e1e added DXGI checks
against Wine's active output state. Replacing DXVK with the GE-Proton11-7
release version avoids the check, but does not correct the output state.

The em11 color-management code requires supports_win_scrgb before marking
an output as HDR. These Windows-specific image descriptions are optional:
the Vulkan driver can present HDR10 through standard Wayland parametric
color management without either Windows-specific feature.

On the affected labwc session, both outputs advertise BT.2020/PQ with
max_lum=10000 and ref_lum=203, and RADV exposes HDR10_ST2084 on Wayland
surfaces. Neither Windows image description is advertised, so Wine
incorrectly reports SDR and DXGI rejects HDR before presentation.

Derive output HDR state from its reported luminance headroom, independently
of the optional presentation path. Keep Windows image-description support
as the gate for Wine's passthrough mapping in vulkan.c; otherwise the
original colorspace is passed to Vulkan WSI. Surface-format support checks,
per-output SDR fallback, and explicit HDR opt-outs remain unchanged.

Downgrade the missing optional-feature messages to traces: their absence
does not imply broken HDR when Vulkan WSI supports the requested colorspace.

Follow-up to the color-management implementation by Etaash Mathamsetty
<45927311+Etaash-mathamsetty@users.noreply.github.com>.

Validation before rebuilding:
- wayland-info: HDR output luminances, no windows_scrgb/windows_bt2100.
- vulkaninfo: RADV exposes HDR10_ST2084 for VK_KHR_wayland_surface.

Manual validation needed after rebuilding:
- Dragon's Dogma 2 with native Wayland and HDR enabled, without HDR flags.
- HDR disabled and mixed SDR/HDR outputs must retain SDR fallback.
- DXVK_NO_HDR=1 and DXVK_HDR=0 must still disable HDR reporting.
- Compositors with Windows image descriptions retain passthrough mapping.
---

diff --git a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_output.c b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_output.c
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_output.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_output.c
@@ -196,8 +196,9 @@ static void wayland_output_done(struct wayland_output *output)
         output->current.ref_lum = output->pending.ref_lum;
     }
 
-    output->current.supports_hdr = process_wayland.supports_win_scrgb &&
-                                    (output->current.max_lum > output->current.ref_lum);
+    /* Output HDR state is independent of the optional Windows image descriptions.
+     * Vulkan WSI can handle HDR colorspaces through standard color management. */
+    output->current.supports_hdr = output->current.max_lum > output->current.ref_lum;
 
     output->current.flags |= output->pending.flags;
     output->pending.flags = 0;
diff --git a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland.c b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland.c
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland.c
@@ -745,10 +745,10 @@ BOOL wayland_process_init(void)
         ERR("Wayland compositor doesn't support xdg_activation_v1! (Window Activation will not be supported)\n");
 
     if (!process_wayland.supports_win_pq)
-        ERR("Wayland compositor doesn't expose windows_bt2100 image description (HDR may look broken)!\n");
+        TRACE("No windows_bt2100 image description; using Vulkan WSI for HDR10.\n");
 
     if (!process_wayland.supports_win_scrgb)
-        ERR("Wayland compositor doesn't expose windows_scrgb image description (HDR may look broken)!\n");
+        TRACE("No windows_scrgb image description; using Vulkan WSI for scRGB.\n");
 
     if (!process_wayland.zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1)
         WARN("Wayland compositor doesn't support optional zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 (HWND dmabuf forwarding won't work)\n");
